---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaRow from "../../components/CtaRow.astro";
import { linkClass } from "../../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Firearm and Accessory Sales Trends in Q2 2025 | FFL Accelerator"
  description="Q2 2025 firearms conversation: less panic, more purpose — carry guns, suppressor talk, and accessories. AR and pumps still slower."
  path="/trends/2025-q2/"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 sm:py-14">
    <p
      class="mb-3 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-gold"
    >
      Trends · Q2 2025
    </p>
    <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">
      Firearm and accessory sales trends in Q2 2025
    </h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      The second quarter of 2025 felt less like a crash and more like a
      market that had stopped pretending every week was 2020. Carry pistols
      and the kit around them still moved. Long guns and bulk ammo did not
      suddenly get interesting again. Treat that as a floor read.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Stabilization is not a rebound</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      “Quieter than the surge, busier than January panic headlines” is how a
      lot of independent shops described June. Purpose-driven tickets —
      something to carry, something to hear-protect, something to train with —
      beat “add another rifle.” Average ticket chatter stayed in the mid-tier
      handgun band. That is qualitative. For a sourced 2025 unit series, see
      the close.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">What kept selling</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Carry guns and the ecosystem.</strong>{" "}
        P365-class and Glock 43X-class demand did not need a new panic. Holsters,
        red dots, and magazines did more work than another duty-size pistol.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Suppressors as a conversation.</strong>{" "}
        Form times and tax-stamp politics were the story on the counter. We are
        not printing an 85% “industry” number we cannot source. If NFA is your
        aisle, sell packages and education — and keep the bound book in real
        compliance software, not the website CMS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Gadgets with a demo.</strong> Chronographs,
        fusion optics, and range toys from the winter show circuit sold when
        someone could try them. They are not a national unit series.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">What stayed soft</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">AR-pattern rifles.</strong> Saturation
        from the early-2020s does not unwind in a quarter. Younger range
        traffic still asked about PCCs and .22s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pump shotguns.</strong> Semis kept winning
        the “home defense, less recoil” sentence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bulk ammo.</strong> Same pattern as Q1:
        this-weekend boxes over pallet cases.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">
      If you mention 2025 units, say which number
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      NSSF, citing Gearfire’s 2025 Annual Industry Report (23 Feb 2026), put
      same-store new firearm units at about <strong class="text-zinc-200"
        >−13% vs 2024</strong
      >.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      If the floor is moving and the website is not, that is the FFL
      Accelerator job. $569 a month. You own the site.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <CtaRow />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-zinc-500">
      Previous:{" "}
      <a href={withBase("trends/2025-q1")} class={linkClass}>Q1 2025 →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
